 expected. The observed-to-expected ratio is 8.8, with a 95% confidence interval of 4.1 to 19.5. December accounts for 14% of the contracts just under the limit, compared with 16% of all its works contracts. The awardee with the most contracts just under the limit, called awardee A here, has 4. Contract [1], for 39,998.53 euros, was awarded in December 2025.\nWhat it cannot show: The high ratio shows a pattern, but it doesn't prove misconduct. Prices near the limit can be due to market rates or budget constraints. The data is a statistical trend, not evidence of improper actions.\nWhat can be checked: Check if multiple contracts cover the same need. Review awardee A's contracts for repeated purchases. Look at contract [1] to see if it aligns with other contracts in terms of scope and timing.","origen_es":"modelo","origen_en":"modelo","modelo":"Qwen3-4B (Q4_K_M, llama.cpp)","regla":"obras","citas":["N8WhFWnZAr0IYE3ZiZ%2BxmQ%3D%3D","rk%2FdrJqHMPFVkTabT%2FRM8A%3D%3D","b7QqiK8nWsu2gkLQ8TeYKA%3D%3D"],"generado":"2026-11-15"}}